steps.body:
Writing `std::` before every name gets noisy in a long function. C++ has two ways to shorten names, and they work very differently.

A **using-declaration** brings one name into the current scope. After `using std::cout;`, plain `cout` means `std::cout`. It behaves like a declaration of that one name: it lasts until the end of its block, and a using-declaration in an inner block hides one from an outer block.

A **using-directive**, `using namespace std;`, declares nothing. It tells lookup to treat every name in `std` as if it were declared in the global namespace, for the rest of the scope. That's hundreds of names, most of which you've never heard of. Declare your own global `count` next to it, and `std::count`, which `<algorithm>` declares, joins the same search:

```cpp error
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int count = 0;  // trades seen today

int main() {
    ++count;
    cout << count << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

g++ 10.2 stops at both uses with `error: reference to 'count' is ambiguous` and lists `std::count` among the candidates. Delete `#include <algorithm>` and the sandbox compiles it, yet Apple clang on a Mac still fails the same way, because libc++'s `<iostream>` happens to bring in `std::count` as well. With a using-directive, whether your code compiles depends on what each library's headers include internally, which changes between versions.

Never put a using-directive in a header. `#include` pastes the header's text into every file that includes it, as *Headers, Translation Units, and the Linker* showed, so all of them get the directive at namespace scope, and none of them can turn it off. A using-declaration inside a function is safe, since its reach ends at the closing brace:

```cpp harness
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

{{code}}

int main() {
    std::cout << tagged("AAPL") << '\n';  // AAPL.O
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::string tagged(std::string symbol) {
    using std::string;  // only inside this function
    const string suffix = ".O";
    return symbol + suffix;
}
```

The usual rule for teams: qualify names in headers, and allow using-declarations, or a directive for your own long namespace, inside a `.cpp` file after all its includes.

