steps.body:
By what you know so far, this call shouldn't compile: `swap` with no `std::` in front, and no `using` anywhere.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <utility>

int main() {
    std::string bid = "bid", ask = "ask";
    swap(bid, ask);  // no std::, and it compiles
    std::cout << bid << ' ' << ask << '\n';  // ask bid
    return 0;
}
```

It works because of **argument-dependent lookup** (ADL): for a function called by an unqualified name, the compiler also searches the namespaces of the arguments' types. `bid` is a `std::string`, so `std` is searched, and `std::swap` turns up. You've relied on it since your first program. `std::cout << "hi"` calls a function named `operator<<` that lives in `std`, found because `std::cout` comes from `std`. Without ADL you'd write `std::operator<<(std::cout, "hi")`.

ADL works for your own types too. Pass a value of an `enum class` declared in `namespace fx`, and `fx` joins the search. Built-in types such as `int` and `double` belong to no namespace, so they add nothing. With two `int`s, plain `swap(a, b)` fails: g++ says `error: 'swap' was not declared in this scope`.

That explains an idiom you'll meet in generic code:

```cpp harness
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <utility>

int main() {
    int a = 1, b = 2;
    {{code}}
    std::cout << a << ' ' << b << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
using std::swap;  // the fallback, for ints and doubles
swap(a, b);       // ADL may still find a better one
```

The using-declaration makes `std::swap` visible, so built-in types work. The call stays unqualified, so ADL can still find a type's own `swap` in its namespace, which may be far cheaper, such as swapping two internal pointers instead of copying data. A using-declaration doesn't switch ADL off: both results go into one set of candidates. Writing `std::swap(a, b)` would skip a type's own `swap` in its namespace. You'll write a `swap` of your own once you have structs and references.

ADL has a cost too: an unqualified call can pick up a function you didn't know about from an argument's namespace. When you mean one exact function, qualify it.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <utility>

namespace fallback {
    std::string label(int value) {
        return "int " + std::to_string(value);
    }
}

namespace fx {
    enum class Ccy { Usd, Eur, Jpy };

    std::string label(Ccy c) {
        switch (c) {
            case Ccy::Usd: return "USD";
            case Ccy::Eur: return "EUR";
            case Ccy::Jpy: return "JPY";
        }
        return "?";
    }
}

int main() {
    std::cout << label(fx::Ccy::Eur) << '\n';  // ADL searches fx
    using fallback::label;
    std::cout << label(42) << '\n';            // the using-declaration
    std::cout << label(fx::Ccy::Jpy) << '\n';  // ADL still wins for a Ccy

    using std::swap;
    int bidQty = 300, askQty = 500;
    swap(bidQty, askQty);                      // std::swap, through the using
    std::string base = "EUR", quote = "USD";
    swap(base, quote);                         // std::swap, through ADL too
    std::cout << bidQty << ' ' << askQty << ' ' << base << '/' << quote << '\n';
    return 0;
}

